drm_atomic_helper: bound the plane fence wait and name what it is stuck on

drm_atomic_helper_wait_for_fences() calls dma_fence_wait(), which is unbounded.
On a Pi 500+ it never returns: the desktop wedges with X parked in

  drm_atomic_helper_disable_plane -> drm_atomic_commit
    -> drm_atomic_helper_commit -> drm_atomic_helper_wait_for_fences
    -> dma_fence_wait -> dma_fence_default_wait   [forever]

while dev.v3d.0.state reports, on every queue, emit == done, outstanding=0,
pending=empty, credit=0, ready=1, paused=0. Every v3d job ever submitted has
completed, so the fence being waited on is not an outstanding GPU job and
nothing will ever come along to complete or reset it.

The hang is completely silent: drm_sched never arms a timeout (nothing is
pending), the GPU raises no interrupt (nothing is running), and an unbounded
wait is not an error to anything in this path. That silence is most of why
this has been so hard to pin down -- there are two doors into it,
v3d_wait_bo_ioctl and this one, and which one a given wedge comes through
varies, so instrumenting only one lets the other pass straight through.

Wait in bounded 5s slices instead, and on each expiry print the fence's driver
name, timeline name, context, seqno and signalled state. A healthy commit pays
nothing -- the fence is already signalled, or signals well inside the first
slice -- and the caller's semantics are unchanged.

What the output distinguishes, which nothing else can:

  signalled=1        the fence fired and the waiter was never woken: a lost
                     wakeup, not a stuck GPU
  foreign driver     the fence belongs to vc4, a dma_fence_array/chain
                     container or a syncobj stub rather than to v3d
  neither            the model of this path is wrong and needs revisiting

FreeBSD-only; the Linux build keeps the original unbounded call.

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_atomic_helper.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_atomic_helper.c
index 40e4e1b..8ca0aa8 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_atomic_helper.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_atomic_helper.c
@@ -1643,7 +1643,53 @@ int drm_atomic_helper_wait_for_fences(struct drm_device *dev,
 		 * still interrupt the operation. Instead of blocking until the
 		 * timer expires, make the wait interruptible.
 		 */
+#ifdef __FreeBSD__
+		/*
+		 * dma_fence_wait() is unbounded, and on a Pi 500+ it never
+		 * returns: the desktop wedges with a thread parked in
+		 * dma_fence_default_wait() forever. dev.v3d.0.state shows every
+		 * v3d queue at emit == done, outstanding=0 -- so the fence is
+		 * not an outstanding GPU job and nothing will come along to
+		 * complete or reset it. Nothing is logged either, because an
+		 * unbounded wait is not an error to anything in this path.
+		 *
+		 * Wait in bounded slices and name what we are waiting on. A
+		 * healthy commit pays nothing: the fence is already signalled
+		 * or signals well inside the first slice.
+		 *
+		 *   signalled=1     the fence fired and the waiter was never
+		 *                   woken -- a lost wakeup, not a stuck GPU
+		 *   foreign driver  the fence is not v3d's at all
+		 */
+		{
+			struct dma_fence *f = new_plane_state->fence;
+			signed long rem;
+			int secs = 0;
+
+			for (;;) {
+				rem = dma_fence_wait_timeout(f, pre_swap,
+							     5L * HZ);
+				if (rem != 0)
+					break;
+				secs += 5;
+				drm_err(dev,
+				    "[PLANE:%d] commit stuck %ds on fence %s/%s ctx %llu seqno %llu signalled=%d\n",
+				    plane->base.id, secs,
+				    (f->ops != NULL &&
+				     f->ops->get_driver_name != NULL) ?
+				    f->ops->get_driver_name(f) : "?",
+				    (f->ops != NULL &&
+				     f->ops->get_timeline_name != NULL) ?
+				    f->ops->get_timeline_name(f) : "?",
+				    (unsigned long long)f->context,
+				    (unsigned long long)f->seqno,
+				    dma_fence_is_signaled(f));
+			}
+			ret = (rem < 0) ? (int)rem : 0;
+		}
+#else
 		ret = dma_fence_wait(new_plane_state->fence, pre_swap);
+#endif
 		if (ret)
 			return ret;
 
